## Short answer  

**No – the `font‑faces` property that you use in a MapLibre Native style is not understood by MapLibre GL JS.**  

GL JS can only get text glyphs in one of three ways:

| Method | How it works | When it is useful |
|--------|--------------|-------------------|
| **`glyphs` URL** (the classic way) | The style points at a server that serves pre‑computed SDF glyph PBFs (`/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf`). | Guarantees identical appearance on GL JS **and** Native; works for every script (including CJK) but requires a glyph server or a copy of the PBF tree. |
| **Omit `glyphs` (or let every range fail) → local‑font fallback** (GL JS ≥ 5.11.0) | GL JS falls back to the browser’s font‑stack resolution (web fonts via `@font-face` or system fonts). The `text‑font` array is treated exactly like a CSS `font-family` list. | Perfect for offline or self‑hosted apps where you can bundle the needed fonts as regular web fonts. CJK is still rendered locally by TinySDF regardless of the list. |
| **`font‑faces` (MapLibre Native only)** | A style‑level map that tells the native runtime which local font file(s) cover which Unicode ranges. | Used when you want to render completely offline on Android/iOS without any network request. **Not available in GL JS**. |

Because GL JS has no `font‑faces` parser, copying that block into a web‑style will be ignored (or cause a validation error) and the map will fall back to the default glyph server (or to the local‑font fallback if `glyphs` is omitted).  

Below is a practical guide on how to achieve the same *offline‑font* experience in GL JS, and what you have to change compared with your Native `font‑faces` setup.

---

## 1. What the `font‑faces` block looks like in Native

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "font-faces": [
    {
      "font-family": "MyBrand",
      "src": "file:///android_asset/fonts/MyBrand-Regular.ttf",
      "unicode-range": "U+0020-00FF"
    },
    {
      "font-family": "MyBrand",
      "src": "file:///android_asset/fonts/MyBrand-CJK.ttf",
      "unicode-range": "U+4E00-9FFF"
    }
  ],
  "layers": [
    {
      "id": "place-label",
      "type": "symbol",
      "layout": {
        "text-font": ["MyBrand Regular"],
        "text-field": "{name}"
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

*Key points*  

* `font-family` is the name you later reference in `text-font`.  
* `unicode-range` tells the native runtime which file to use for which code points.  
* The files live on the device, so no network request is needed.

---

## 2. How GL JS can render *offline* fonts

### 2.1. Omit the `glyphs` field (or let it 404)

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  // No "glyphs": "https://…/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf"
  "layers": [
    {
      "id": "place-label",
      "type": "symbol",
      "layout": {
        "text-font": ["MyBrand Regular", "Noto Sans Regular"],
        "text-field": "{name}"
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

*What happens*  

* GL JS treats each entry in `text-font` exactly like a CSS `font-family` list.  
* For every code point it tries to draw, the browser looks for a matching glyph in the **first** loaded font that contains it.  
* If the browser cannot find a glyph (e.g., the font file is missing or the script isn’t covered), the character is rendered with the generic system fallback (often a “tofu” box).  

### 2.2. Load the fonts as **web fonts** (bundled with your app)

```html
<link rel="preload" href="/fonts/MyBrand-Regular.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin>
<link rel="preload" href="/fonts/MyBrand-CJK.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin>

<style>
@font-face {
  font-family: "MyBrand Regular";
  src: url("/fonts/MyBrand-Regular.woff2") format("woff2");
  font-weight: normal;
  font-style: normal;
  unicode-range: U+0020-00FF;          /* optional – helps the browser skip the file for other code points */
}
@font-face {
  font-family: "MyBrand Regular";      /* same family name, different file */
  src: url("/fonts/MyBrand-CJK.woff2") format("woff2");
  font-weight: normal;
  font-style: normal;
  unicode-range: U+4E00-9FFF;          /* CJK range */
}
</style>
```

*Why this works*  

* The `unicode-range` descriptor in `@font-face` **mirrors** what you had in `font-faces`. The browser will only download (or keep in memory) the file when it actually needs a glyph from that range.  
* Because the fonts are served from the same origin (or a CORS‑enabled CDN), the whole map can work offline after the files are cached (e.g., via a Service Worker).  

### 2.3. Optional: Use the FontFace API for dynamic loading

If you prefer to load fonts programmatically (e.g., after a user toggles a theme), you can do:

```js
const myBrand = new FontFace(
  "MyBrand Regular",
  "url(/fonts/MyBrand-Regular.woff2)",
  { unicodeRange: "U+0020-00FF" }
);
await myBrand.load();
document.fonts.add(myBrand);

const myBrandCJK = new FontFace(
  "MyBrand Regular",
  "url(/fonts/MyBrand-CJK.woff2)",
  { unicodeRange: "U+4E00-9FFF" }
);
await myBrandCJK.load();
document.fonts.add(myBrandCJK);
```

After the fonts are added to `document.fonts`, GL JS will automatically pick them up when it renders symbols.

### 2.4. CJK handling in GL JS

* **By default** GL JS renders CJK characters *locally* with TinySDF, **ignoring** the `text-font` list (except for the optional `weight` keyword). This is controlled by the map option `localIdeographFontFamily`.  
* If you *do* want to use your own CJK web font (e.g., a brand‑specific Noto CJK variant), set the option:

```js
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: "map",
  style: "style.json",
  localIdeographFontFamily: '"MyBrand CJK", sans-serif' // any CSS font-family string
});
```

* Setting `localIdeographFontFamily: false` disables the TinySDF shortcut and forces GL JS to request glyph PBFs for CJK. This is **slow** and usually unnecessary unless you need the exact glyph outlines from your own font.

### 2.5. RTL scripts (Arabic, Hebrew, etc.)

GL JS does **not** shape right‑to‑left text automatically. You must load the RTL text plugin **once**, before creating the map:

```js
import { setRTLTextPlugin } from "maplibre-gl";

setRTLTextPlugin(
  "https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl-rtl-text.js",
  null,
  true // set to true to load synchronously (recommended for offline)
);
```

After the plugin is installed, the same `text-font` list you use for Latin will be consulted for Arabic/Hebrew glyphs. The plugin does the necessary character reordering; the actual glyph shapes still come from the font you provide.

### 2.6. Scripts that need shaping (Devanagari, Khmer, etc.)

GL JS **does not** run a shaping engine (HarfBuzz, Raqm, etc.). Even if you supply a font that contains the required glyphs, the renderer will place each Unicode code point side‑by‑side, which produces broken or missing conjuncts for many Indic scripts. At the moment there is **no configuration fix**; you would need to:

* Accept the limitation (show the text in a fallback Latin script), **or**  
* Pre‑render the labels as images/SVGs on the server and use them as icons, **or**  
* Wait for a future GL JS feature that integrates a shaping library (currently under discussion but not scheduled).

---

## 3. Migration checklist – from Native `font‑faces` to GL JS

| Native `font‑faces` feature | GL JS equivalent / action |
|-----------------------------|---------------------------|
| `font-family` name          | Use the **same string** in `text-font` and in `@font-face` `font-family`. |
| `src` (local file path)    | Bundle the font as a **web font** (`.woff2`/`.ttf`) and serve it from your web server (or embed it in the app bundle). |
| `unicode-range`            | Add the same `unicode-range` descriptor to the `@font-face` rule (optional but recommended). |
| Per‑file Unicode ranges    | No need to split the file for GL JS; you can keep a single file that covers all ranges, but splitting reduces download size. |
| Offline‑only rendering      | Omit the `glyphs` field **and** make sure the web fonts are cached (Service Worker, `preload`, or `Cache-Control`). |
| CJK local‑ideograph handling| Use `localIdeographFontFamily` if you want a custom on‑device CJK font; otherwise leave default. |
| RTL shaping                 | Load the RTL text plugin (`setRTLTextPlugin`). |
| Complex‑script shaping (Devanagari, Khmer, etc.) | **Not supported** – you must use a workaround (pre‑rendered icons, server‑side rasterization). |
| Cross‑platform identical appearance | **Only guaranteed** if you keep a `glyphs` server (or copy the same PBF tree) and *do not* rely on the local‑font fallback. |

---

## 4. Example: Full GL JS style + HTML skeleton

**style.json**

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "sources": {
    "osm": {
      "type": "vector",
      "url": "https://.../tileserver"
    }
  },
  "layers": [
    {
      "id": "place-label",
      "type": "symbol",
      "source": "osm",
      "source-layer": "place",
      "layout": {
        "text-field": "{name}",
        "text-font": ["MyBrand Regular", "Noto Sans Regular"],
        "text-size": 14,
        "text-anchor": "center"
      },
      "paint": {
        "text-color": "#222"
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

**index.html**

```html
<!doctype html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <title>Offline fonts with MapLibre GL JS</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@5.12.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet">
  <style>
    html, body, #map { margin:0; height:100%; width:100%; }
    @font-face {
      font-family: "MyBrand Regular";
      src: url("./fonts/MyBrand-Regular.woff2") format("woff2");
      unicode-range: U+0020-00FF;
    }
    @font-face {
      font-family: "MyBrand Regular";
      src: url("./fonts/MyBrand-CJK.woff2") format("woff2");
      unicode-range: U+4E00-9FFF;
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script type="module">
import { Map, setRTLTextPlugin } from "https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@5.12.0/dist/maplibre-gl.esm.js";

// RTL plugin (optional, but required for Arabic/Hebrew)
setRTLTextPlugin(
  "https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@5.12.0/dist/maplibre-gl-rtl-text.js",
  null,
  true
);

const map = new Map({
  container: "map",
  style: "./style.json",
